621935446.en:
Consider the following (clearly unsound) program:
```rust
unsafe fn main(){
    let x: u8 = pick();
    println("{x}");
     if x==47 { unreachable_unchecked() }
     println!("bar");
     loop {}
}
```
There is an interesting consequence of time-travelling UB that does not require the compiler to know that println is willreturn - only that it has access to a decision oracle of when println diverges or not.
The compiler can pick whatever value it wants for x, so naturally it decides to pick 47. The catch is, now it has to print 47. This can be slow, since it now has to store 47 to memory.
With time travelling, however, the compiler already knows about UB, so the first println can just print garbage (allowing it to elide the memory store), even if it might diverge - in the case it diverges, the compiler picked whatever value was printed. If it didn't it picked 47, and the program hits UB.
Are we sure nothing does this optimization without creating the appropriate memory store?

