622100057.en:
Anyways, attempting to express a more elaborated version of the result:
```rust
let x = 47;
let y = pick::<u8>();
println!("{y}"); 
if x == 47 { unreachable_unchecked(); }
// ...
```

The idea is that in environments where the value of `x` matters (rather than just the value printed), the compiler "just" picked 47, hit UB, and printed the wrong thing.

@_**RalfJ|120791** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Interesting.20Consequence.20of.20time.20traveling.20UB/near/622099353):
```quote
?!?? are you trying to be deliberately annoying or something? "every execution of the transformed program" obviously refers to the executions that can occur. come on.
```

"only the executions that actually occur" is the load-bearing part of the situation. If we consider this optimization not as an IR-IR transformation, but as a mapping from some higher-level IR to some lower-level IR, the transformation is ultimately allowed to eliminate certain executions from the result. Specifically, there's only two "classes" of  environment-execution pairs that matter here: One where `println!()` terminates, in which case the value chosen for x (or y) does not matter, and one where `println!()` returns, we hit UB, the entire result of the execution  is UB (under time-travelling), and The fact the console happens to show "42" is just a consequence of UB.

