621813455.en:
Split from https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Do.20we.20already.20have.20overlapping.20allocations.3F/near/621807223. This is more of an unsafe-code-guidelines question than opsem (since I don't really think there's a way for us to fix this in opsem...) but that channel became this one, so... I'm putting it here. Feel free to move this to somewhere else if that's preferable.

In some cases, it's desirable to forbid the optimizer from being able to see through an indirect call (as a sort of compiler barrier, but not in a way that the normal `compiler_fence()` or `asm!` fences do). For example, in https://github.com/RustCrypto/utils/issues/1472, I suggest an approach to ensuring that code from a closure stays between enablement and disablement of DIT (an aarch64 CPU flag that controls whether or not instructions are constant time with respect to their inputs). The approach I suggest is basically:

```rs
pub fn with_dit_enabled<R, F: FnOnce() -> R>(f: F) -> R {
    enable_dit();
    let _guard = scope_guard(|| disable_dit());
    let mut o: Option<F> = Some(f);
    // Could be unwrap_unchecked, but I like that this doesn't have unsafe.
    let f: &mut dyn FnMut() -> R = &mut || o.take().unwrap()();
    let indirect_f = core::hint::black_box(f);
    indirect_f()
}
```

This essentially rewrites a direct call into one which is forced to be indirect. The issue is that the compiler is allowed to (and can, if PGO is enabled) turn indirect calls into conditional direct calls, e.g. it can turn:

```rs
// Before:
// Pretend `indirect` is just `fn()`, not `&mut dyn FnMut()` like above -- I don't think
// it matters here
indirect();
```

Into:
```rs
// After:
// Pretend `direct` is the static function that is equivalent to `indirect`.
if (core::ptr::fn_addr_eq(indirect, direct)) {
	direct();
} else {
	indirect();
}
```

Then, `direct()` could (presumably) be inlined, and have its code moved outside of the DIT section, which is bad news. This is problematic because AFAIK there's no other way of actually keeping the code from escaping the section. I guess `#[optimize(none)]`? (Or perhaps `#[inline(never)]` and hoping interprocedural opts don't do too much?). That seems... rather unfortunate.

Aside: A similar thing happens with some implementations of zeroizing (where you store the zeroing function to call, allowing you to have an optimized zeroing implementation, rather than using volatile writes). This is less concerning, since there are ways to prevent this (by just using volatile writes).

(CC @**Nikita Popov** since this is related to the link just provided)

