611770145.en:
Per [discussion in #157428](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/157428#discussion_r3617493334), we need to figure out how to enable custom allocators to integrate with LLVM's new semantics for allocators & the special-case code we currently ship (namely, `noalias` on `Box<T, A>` when `A = Global`). That PR proposes the following traits relevant to this conversation:
```rust
/// # Safety
/// - Can't unwind out of (de/re)allocating methods
/// - The compiler might add or remove matching pairs of `allocate`/`deallocate`
/// (also other things, but irrelevant rn)
unsafe trait Allocator {}

/// # Safety
/// - Absolutely nothing will ever cause an allocation from this allocator to be invalidated except explicitly calling `deallocate` (or a reallocating method), even if its lifetime expires, it's dropped, it's not dropped, etc.
unsafe trait StaticAllocator: Allocator {}

/// # Safety
/// - This allocator isn't reentrant in `std` (or else it'll blow the stack, but it's not language UB necessarily otherwise)
unsafe trait GlobalAllocator: StaticAllocator + Sync + 'static
```
this is not quite enough to justify the `noalias` flag on `Box` alone. AIUI, we currently take advantage of the fact that LLVM offers some special-casing for the global allocator that's defined with `#[global_allocator]`, and the requirements on `StaticAllocator` above are enough to justify said special-casing. in other words, one option to justify that `noalias` would be:
```rust
#[lang = "native_allocator"] // enable LLVM special-casing
struct NativeAllocator<A: StaticAllocator>(A);
// in codegen, emit `noalias` for `Box<T, A>` where `A = NativeAllocator<Whatever>`
```
another option (what I initially suggested) was an `#[lang = "..."] unsafe trait NoaliasAllocator: Allocator {}` with safety requirements amounting to "manually ensure you respect noalias semantics for the pointers passed back to de/reallocating methods", which we *also* implemented for `Global` with a big warning that "something being suitable as a global allocator doesn't always mean you can also impl `NoaliasAllocator` on it yourself". this would then mean that LLVM special-case magic is separate from noalias (since, AIUI, LLVM also requires the semantics of `StaticAllocator`).

i guess i'd like a vibecheck from opsem on this; what's the right way to go here without making your job hell to define semantics? and/or is there some perspective I missed here?

